Configure Router R1:
--------------------
    (Interface G0/0):
    -----------------
    Router>enable
    Router#conf
    Router#configure ter
    Router#configure terminal
    Enter configuration commands, one per line.  End with CNTL/Z.
    Router(config)#int
    Router(config)#interface gi
    Router(config)#interface gigabitEthernet 0/0
    Router(config-if)#ip add
    Router(config-if)#ip address 192.168.10.1 255.255.255.0
    Router(config-if)#no shu
    Router(config-if)#no shutdown
    Router(config-if)#
    %LINK-5-CHANGED: Interface GigabitEthernet0/0, changed state to up
    %LINEPROTO-5-UPDOWN: Line protocol on Interface GigabitEthernet0/0, changed state to up
    Router(config-if)#
    Router(config-if)#ex
    Router(config-if)#exit
    Router(config)#
    Router(config)#
    Router(config)#
    Router(config)#host
    Router(config)#hostname Router1
    Router1(config)#end

    (Interface G0/1):
    -----------------
    Router1#enable
    Router1#conf
    Router1#configure ter
    Router1#configure terminal
    Enter configuration commands, one per line.  End with CNTL/Z.
    Router1(config)#int
    Router1(config)#interface gi
    Router1(config)#interface gigabitEthernet 0/1
    Router1(config-if)#ip address 10.10.10.1 255.255.255.252
    Router1(config-if)#no shut
    Router1(config-if)#no shutdown
    Router1(config-if)#
    %LINK-5-CHANGED: Interface GigabitEthernet0/1, changed state to up
    Router1(config-if)#end
    Router1#
    %SYS-5-CONFIG_I: Configured from console by console

    Commands:
    =========
    enable: enters Privileged EXEC Mode, where you can view and modify the router configuration
    configure terminal: Enter Global Configuration Mode, where you can configure interface, routing, VLANs, and other settings
    interface gigabitEthernet 0/0:  We are telling the router: I want to configure interface G0/0
    ip address 192.168.10.1 255.255.255.0:  This assigns the gateway IP address for the 192.168.10.0/24 LAN
    no shutdown:  By default, Cisco router interface are administratively down
    exit: Exit from G0/0 configuration mode back to global configuration mode

    interface gigabitEthernet 0/1:
    (We're now selecting the interface connected to Router R2)
    ip address 10.10.10.1 255.255.255.252 (This gives R1 its IP address on the point-to-point link)

    Router1#sh ip interface brief
    Interface              IP-Address      OK? Method Status                Protocol
    GigabitEthernet0/0     192.168.10.1    YES manual up                    up
    GigabitEthernet0/1     10.10.10.1      YES manual up                    down
    GigabitEthernet0/2     unassigned      YES unset  administratively down down
    Vlan1                  unassigned      YES unset  administratively down down
    Router1#

    Understand "Status" and "Protocol":
    -----------------------------------
    Status = Physical Layer (Layer1): Is the cable connected, Yes(Status=UP)
    Protocol = Data Link Layer (Layer2): Can I actually communicate with the device on the other side: (Protocol=UP)


    Additional Information:
    =======================
    R2 G0/1: what is connected here:
    --------------------------------
    R1 G0/1  <------------->  R2 G0/1 [R1 G0/1  <------------->  R2 G0/1]

    The network is: 10.10.10.0/30
    Usable IPs are:
    Network ID      : 10.10.10.0
    R1              : 10.10.10.1
    R2              : 10.10.10.2
    Broadcast       : 10.10.10.3

    So:
    R2 G0/1
    IP Address : 10.10.10.2
    Subnet Mask : 255.255.255.252

    Topology:
    ---------
    PC1 -- SW2 -- R2 G0/0

    Q) What network does PC1 belong to: Earlier we configured PC1 s:
        IP Address: 192.168.20.20
